17592186478904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479054 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479054 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479054 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186478708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479054 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fc384e98-6294-5707-b8e0-2ed99c0215ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479092 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479092 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110601" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479092 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479092 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:4x+5y-z=0\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{0\\}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479092 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5d0c1517-fefb-518c-9505-969066e0bce6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479092 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $4x+5y-z=0$ since $4\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 0-0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $A.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ since ${0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. This subset has only one element, the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩,$ which is a special subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479117 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479117 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110606" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479117 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479117 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:3x+4y=1\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x+3y=0\\}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479117 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ada9b840-b530-5081-8048-c09da4df32f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479117 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ consists only of the vector $⟨0,0⟩$, which is a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $3x+4y=1$ since $3\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 0\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. It also represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x+3y=0$ since $0+3\\cdot 0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $C.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
